+# SMS sign-in integration
+
+The site is static. It defaults to an explicitly labeled interactive preview that sends no SMS and creates no session. Request a code and use **123456** to try Rewards or Order online. The preview expires codes after five minutes, permits five attempts, and offers resend after 30 seconds. None of these client-side limits provide production security.
+
+To enable real SMS, set `window.DAILY_GRIND_AUTH = { mode: 'live', csrfToken: 'SERVER_ISSUED_TOKEN' }` in your server-rendered page before `auth.js` runs. Provide these same-origin JSON endpoints:
+
+- `POST /api/auth/sms/request` receives `{ phone, intent }`, with an E.164 phone and `rewards` or `order` intent. Return `{ challengeId, expiresIn: 300 }` after accepting the SMS for delivery.
+- `POST /api/auth/sms/verify` receives `{ challengeId, code, intent }`. Verify the challenge server-side, consume it atomically, set a Secure, HttpOnly, SameSite session cookie, and return `{ verified: true }`. The client then navigates to `/rewards` or `/order-online`.
+- Return non-2xx JSON with `error: "invalid_code"` or `"expired_code"` where appropriate. HTTP 429 displays a rate-limit message. Other failures display a general error; do not return account-existence details.
+
+The backend must generate cryptographically random six-digit codes, bind challenges to a phone/session, expire them, enforce attempt and resend limits, invalidate earlier codes on resend, validate Origin/CSRF, and never trust client validation. Never log codes or retain them in plaintext. The sample CSRF header is `X-CSRF-Token`; configure the server accordingly. Keep the provider credentials on the server.
+
+Use HTTPS. Send human-readable text followed by a final origin-bound line, using the actual deployed hostname without protocol, port, or path:
+
+```text
+Your Daily Grind sign-in code is 012345. It expires in 5 minutes.
+
+@your-deployed-hostname.example #012345
+```
+
+WebOTP is feature-detected and listens during the active code step, before the send request. Requests are aborted on manual submit, resend, phone change, navigation, and a two-minute timeout. Dismissal or unavailable WebOTP leaves native `autocomplete="one-time-code"` suggestions and manual entry intact. The single text input preserves leading zeros and supports paste. A server failure never produces a signed-in UI.
+
+The existing menu, locations, policy, Rewards, and ordering routes remain links for the host application to provide. No development server or browser verification was used.
